{
  "text": " You've been very important in AI in the last couple years. I mean, I think people remember seeing you in the headlines when you came to Meta. I think this, you know, isn't brand new to you. But in the last couple weeks, you have designed and released a product that is being used by more people than ChatGPT was in its first couple weeks. It feels like you have been thrust into a new kind of part of this conversation. Where, frankly, people will be asking you more questions like, where exactly, Alex, do you fall on this spectrum? Like, what would you want to see? Because you're one of the people responsible. Do you feel like you've come to those conclusions yet? Yeah, I think like anyone who works on this technology, these are things that I've like been thinking about for years and years. Truly the way I think about what I want to do and what I want to accomplish and how I want to shape this conversation is like enable everybody to have a common dialogue and then get to the right place collectively. Like, I don't think at this point in the conversation, it's like that helpful for me to say, oh, I believe this. And, you know, you all should listen to me because, you know, I built Muse or whatever. We already have a bunch of people. They all believe slightly different things. Ultimately, this is a technology that we need, like, collective action on that everyone needs to agree on that we all need to get to the right place. So what I really care about is, like, how do we facilitate and have the relevant dialogues in public that and private that enable us to get to the point where we all agree on a path? And one of the very strange things for me is, like, I know a lot of these people and I've met them. They're all brilliant. But the degree to which certain personal animosities are, like, actually defining what people say and do and, like, how they're developing this really important technology is insane to me. It is, in some ways, a very human story that, like, feuds or, like, just the fact that, like, I really don't trust this other person can, can, like, snowball into this, like, civilizational level problem. But I don't think that serves any of us. Like, I think, ultimately, we have to all put our differences aside, put our histories aside, and just figure out how we solve this together. It sounds very general, but that's a fairly specific ask. I mean, there are constraints that prevent that right now. There are antitrust regulations that prevent a lot of those conversations from happening. And one of the proposals, one piece of many proposals that Dario put forward is waivers from those antitrust laws that would allow those kinds of conversations. I mean, tell me if I'm wrong if there are secret conversations happening. But I don't think there is, first, the agreed-upon group in the United States that's having that conversation. And then, also, like, how do we even begin to have those conversations with China? But, actually, what don't I know? Is that something that's happening in, you know, some private room somewhere? Or is it that, like, we're still at the beginning of that and we need to figure out how to do it? I don't think it's happening right now. At the White House, there was this dialogue and these accords put forth, which are a real step. Like, I think there's getting everyone to agree to something is, like, that's really big. Everyone agrees on something. And I think that thing, which is basically saying, hey, we're going to, every company is going to set out and develop what their internal controls and principles around developing this technology look like. And there's going to be multiple layers of verification on that, from internal teams that verify to external auditors that validate that they're following those controls to board-level committees that ensure that you're following those controls. Like, I think that's a great step. Because in many ways, a lot of what's needed right now is just transparency. Like, we just need to ensure that, you know, people are going about this sanely and rationally. And I think in some ways, like, people don't even trust that necessarily. As an industry, we will have trust-building to do. I am an optimist. I think you're an optimist. Like, I think we're all optimists. Like, I really do think these conversations will naturally happen. This last accord that I referenced, like, that was in the White House. The government helped facilitate that. And so, I think we're going to be seeing a lot more of that. It's going to be bumpy. There will be moments of total confusion and chaos and, like, what the heck is going on. But I see progress, too.",
